    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "We present a field example of an analogue monoclinal reservoir and present methods to quantify both fold geometry and fracture characteristics and compare the relationships between the two. A highly accurate Light Detection and Ranging (LiDAR) data set supplies ~3.5x107 x, y, z data points of the topography of the exhumed Raplee Ridge monocline. Through computer and field based mapping we identify bedding planes of five stratigraphic layer as constraints on the fold geometry. These data are then interpolated using a minimum curvature spline (MCS) algorithm to create complete fold models at multiple stratigraphic levels. After calculating curvature values of the models, field measured fracture characteristics are compared to both modeled absolute maximum curvature measured fold limb dip values. We show that folding related fracture density increases with fold limb dip, while layer curvature seems to be uncorrelated.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
